
Topic modeling is a well established technique  in Natural Language Processing (NLP). Ever since the introduction of the LDA, it has been a powerful tool for splitting large datasets into different categories or "topics" based on their content. Traditional techniques usually perform this modeling by observing word frequencies in the documents. This technique has shown limited abilities to handles documents riddles with noise or very varied language More modern approaches instead attempt to harness the contextual abilities of LLMs such as BERT, to capture semantic meaning from the texts. This thesis investigate how the more modern approaches in topic modeling compare to the traditional ones. Using a Historical dataset from Riksarkivet that is abundant in noise due to OCR transcriptions, contains a very varied language due to language drift over time, as well as being primarily in Swedish, 4 different topic models are evaluated against each other. the models evaluated are LDA, ETM, CTM and BERTopic. The results of the study show that models able to utilize semantic context perform better on the dataset at Riksarkivet compared to traditional models. The more modern models, usually does not need to to apply heavy preprocessing to remove stop words like the traditional models. This study however also show that in the case of BERTopic, removing the stopwords actually leads to improved results, despite stopwords being important for semantic structure.
